(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_chemicalcoordinationandintegration=[{id:"neet-2026r-q092",pn:92,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Pancreatic islet hormones",q:"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["\u03B1-cells of pancreas secrete insulin","Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis","\u03B2-cells of pancreas secrete insulin","\u03B1-cells of pancreas secrete glucagon"],c:0,s:`The two main cell types of the islets of Langerhans are \u03B1-cells and \u03B2-cells.
\u03B1-cells secrete glucagon, while \u03B2-cells secrete insulin.
Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis, while insulin stimulates glycogenesis.
So the statement that \u03B1-cells secrete insulin is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q094",pn:94,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Pituitary gland",q:`Which of the following statements related to pituitary gland are correct?
(a) It is divided anatomically into adenohypophysis and neurohypophysis
(b) It secretes follicle stimulating hormone
(c) It secretes melanocyte stimulating hormone
(d) It does not secrete prolactin
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a), (b) and (c) only","(c) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only","(a) and (b) only"],c:0,s:`Adenohypophysis of the pituitary gland also secretes prolactin, so (d) is wrong.
(a), (b) and (c) are correct.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q157",pn:157,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones and functions",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Excess growth hormone \u2014 I. Reabsorption of water and electrolytes in kidney
B. Luteinizing hormone \u2014 II. Contraction of uterus during child birth
C. Vasopressin \u2014 III. Acromegaly
D. Oxytocin \u2014 IV. Ovulation
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],c:0,s:`Excess growth hormone \u2192 Acromegaly
Luteinizing hormone \u2192 Ovulation
Vasopressin \u2192 Reabsorption of water and electrolytes in kidneys
Oxytocin \u2192 Contraction of uterus during child birth`},{id:"neet-2026-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones and functions",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Cortisol \u2014 I. Stimulates the formation of alveoli in mammary glands
B. Aldosterone \u2014 II. Produces anti-inflammatory reactions
C. Cholecystokinin \u2014 III. Stimulates reabsorption of Na\u207A and water from renal tubule
D. Progesterone \u2014 IV. Stimulates secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],c:1,s:`Cortisol produces anti-inflammatory reactions and suppresses the immune response (A-II).
Aldosterone acts mainly at the renal tubules and stimulates reabsorption of Na\u207A and water, and excretion of K\u207A and phosphate ions (B-III).
Cholecystokinin (CCK) acts on both pancreas and gall bladder and stimulates secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice (C-IV).
Progesterone acts on the mammary glands and stimulates the formation of alveoli (D-I).
So the correct option is (2).`},{id:"neet-2025-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones of different organs",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Heart \u2014 I. Erythropoietin
B. Kidney \u2014 II. Aldosterone
C. Gastro-intestinal tract \u2014 III. Atrial natriuretic factor
D. Adrenal Cortex \u2014 IV. Secretin
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],c:3,s:`Heart secretes atrial natriuretic factor.
Kidney secretes erythropoietin.
Gastro-intestinal tract secretes secretin.
Adrenal cortex secretes aldosterone.`},{id:"neet-2025-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Adrenal medulla",q:`Consider the following statements regarding function of adrenal medullary hormones:
(A) It causes pupilary constriction.
(B) It is a hyperglycemic hormone.
(C) It causes piloerection.
(D) It increases strength of heart contraction.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["C and D only","B, C and D only","A, C and D only","D only"],c:1,s:`The adrenal medulla secretes adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline (norepinephrine), called emergency hormones.
Both hormones cause pupillary dilation (not constriction).
They stimulate breakdown of glycogen, which raises blood glucose, i.e., cause hyperglycemia.
They cause piloerection (raising of hair).
They increase the strength of heart contraction, i.e., heartbeat.`},{id:"neet-2025-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Insulin",q:"Why can\u2019t insulin be given orally to diabetic patients?",o:["Human body will elicit strong immune response","It will be digested in Gastro-Intestinal (GI) tract","Because of structural variation","Its bioavailability will be increased"],c:1,s:`Insulin is a protein molecule.
So if given orally it will be digested in the gastro-intestinal tract.`},{id:"neet-2025-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Pituitary hormones",q:"Which of the following hormones released from the pituitary is actually synthesized in the hypothalamus?",o:["Luteinizing hormone (LH)","Anti-diuretic hormone (ADH)","Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)","Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)"],c:1,s:`The neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary, pars nervosa) stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin (also called ADH, antidiuretic hormone).
These are actually synthesised by the hypothalamus and carried along axons to the neurohypophysis.
The pars distalis (anterior pituitary) makes FSH, ACTH and LH.`},{id:"neet-2025-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones and their sources",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Progesterone \u2014 I. Pars intermedia
B. Relaxin \u2014 II. Ovary
C. Melanocyte stimulating hormone \u2014 III. Adrenal Medulla
D. Catecholamines \u2014 IV. Corpus luteum
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],c:0,s:`The correct answer is A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III.
Progesterone: a steroid hormone secreted by the corpus luteum.
Relaxin: a protein hormone secreted by the ovaries in the later stage of pregnancy.
Melanocyte stimulating hormone: a protein hormone released by the pars intermedia.
Catecholamines: amino-acid derived hormones released from the adrenal medulla in emergency conditions.`},{id:"neet-2024-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormone chemistry",q:"Which of the following is not a steroid hormone?",o:["Progesterone","Glucagon","Cortisol","Testosterone"],c:1,s:`Glucagon, made by the pancreas, is a protein hormone.
Progesterone, cortisol and testosterone are steroid hormones.`},{id:"neet-2024-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Gonadotropins and gonadal hormones",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : FSH acts upon ovarian follicles in female and Leydig cells in male.
Reason R : Growing ovarian follicles secrete estrogen in female while interstitial cells secrete androgen in male human being.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."],c:1,s:`Option (2) is correct.
FSH is a gonadotropin that acts on ovarian follicles and makes them grow.
In males, LH (not FSH) acts on Leydig cells, causing androgen secretion.
Growing follicles do secrete estrogen, and interstitial cells do secrete androgen.
So the Assertion is false and the Reason is true.`},{id:"neet-2024-q191",pn:191,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Disorders of endocrine glands",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Exophthalmic goiter \u2014 I. Excess secretion of cortisol, moon face and hyperglycemia
B. Acromegaly \u2014 II. Hypo-secretion of thyroid hormone and stunted growth.
C. Cushing's syndrome \u2014 III. Hyper secretion of thyroid hormone and protruding eye balls.
D. Cretinism \u2014 IV. Excessive secretion of growth hormone.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],c:1,s:`Exophthalmic goiter \u2013 III: too much thyroid hormone and protruding eyeballs.
Acromegaly \u2013 IV: excess growth hormone.
Cushing's syndrome \u2013 I: excess cortisol, moon face and hyperglycemia.
Cretinism \u2013 II: too little thyroid hormone and stunted growth.`},{id:"neet-2023-q184",pn:184,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones of GI tract, heart and kidney",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I: A. CCK; B. GIP; C. ANF; D. ADH
List II: I. Kidney; II. Heart; III. Gastric gland; IV. Pancreas
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],c:1,s:`CCK acts on the gall bladder and pancreas, so A-IV.
GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility, so B-III.
ANF is released from the atrial wall of the heart, so C-II.
ADH acts on the kidney (distal tubules) to increase water reabsorption, so D-I.`},{id:"neet-2023-q188",pn:188,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Thyroid hormones",q:`Which of the following are NOT under the control of thyroid hormone?
A. Maintenance of water and electrolyte balance
B. Regulation of basal metabolic rate
C. Normal rhythm of sleep-wake cycle
D. Development of immune system
E. Support the process of RBCs formation
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["D and E only","A and D only","B and C only","C and D only"],c:3,s:`Thyroid hormones regulate BMR, water and electrolyte balance and support RBC formation.
They do not control the sleep-wake rhythm or immune system development.
So C and D are not under their control.`}]})();
